    reason: 'Peak crying at 6–8 weeks is normal and it ends. If you feel you might snap, put the baby down safely and step away: that is the right thing to do, not a failure.',
    minutes: 3,
    ageMonthsMin: 0,
    ageMonthsMax: 3,
    paragraphs: ['In the first three months, your baby will cry more than at any other point in their life. This is not a sign that something is wrong with them, or with you.', 'Crying peaks at around six to eight weeks and then begins to settle. Researchers call this the period of PURPLE crying; the letters stand for the features that make it so hard, and it Peaks around two months, it comes Unexpectedly, the baby Resists soothing, they look like they\'re in Pain even when they\'re not, it goes on for a Long time, and it happens most in the Evening.', 'That evening timing is why so many families find 5pm to 9pm the hardest stretch of the day. It has a name in many Indian homes, the "sanjh ka rona", and grandmothers have described it for generations.', 'Try one thing at a time and give it a few minutes before switching. Constant switching between remedies means nothing gets a chance to work.', 'Hold them upright against your chest, skin to skin if you can. Walk while you hold them: the movement matters more than the walking. Try a firm swaddle. Shush loudly near their ear, louder than you think is polite; the womb was not a quiet place. Take them outside: a change of air and light resets some babies completely.', 'Gripe water and ghutti are widely used and widely recommended by relatives. Many commercial preparations contain alcohol, sugar or unlisted herbs, and some traditional home preparations carry a real risk of contamination. There is no strong evidence that any of them reduce crying. If you want to use something, ask your paediatrician which specific product is safe rather than accepting a general recommendation.', 'Never shake a baby, however desperate you feel. If you reach the point where you might, put the baby down somewhere safe, walk into another room, and let them cry for five minutes while you breathe. A crying baby in a cot is safe. A shaken baby is not. This is the single most important sentence in this guide.', 'Crying that sounds different from usual: high-pitched, weak, or moaning. A fever above 38°C. Refusing feeds. Fewer wet nappies than usual. Vomiting that is green or forceful. A baby who is unusually floppy or unusually stiff. Trust yourself here; you know their normal better than anyone.', 'You will feel, at some point in these months, that you cannot do this. That feeling is not evidence. It is exhaustion talking. It passes, and so does the crying.'],
