    title: 'Is My Baby Getting Enough Milk?',
    reason: 'Six or more wet nappies a day and steady weight gain are your real evidence. Soft breasts, frequent feeding and evening cluster feeding are normal, not warning signs.',
    minutes: 3,
    ageMonthsMin: 0,
    ageMonthsMax: 3,
    paragraphs: ['This is the question that keeps new mothers awake more than any other, and the anxiety is made worse by the fact that you cannot see how much has gone in.', 'Nappies tell you more than anything else. From day five onwards, expect six or more heavy wet nappies in twenty-four hours, and at least three yellow, soft stools a day in the early weeks. That is the clearest daily evidence you have.', 'Weight is the other measure. Babies lose up to seven to ten percent of their birth weight in the first days and should be back to birth weight by two weeks. After that, steady gain along their own line on the growth chart: not the same line as anyone else\'s baby.', 'Beyond that: they feed eight to twelve times in twenty-four hours, you can hear or see swallowing, your breast feels softer after a feed, and they are alert and settled for at least some part of the day.', 'A baby who feeds very often. A baby who cluster feeds all evening. A baby who wakes at night. Breasts that feel soft after the first few weeks: that is your supply regulating, not dropping. Not being able to express much: a pump is far less efficient than a baby. A baby who takes a bottle after a feed; babies will almost always suck more if offered, because sucking is a reflex.', 'Every one of these is normally described as a problem by someone in the family, and none of them is.', 'In the evenings, especially around three weeks, six weeks and three months, your baby may feed almost continuously for hours. It feels like starvation. It is actually how they increase your supply for a growth spurt. It lasts a few days. Eat, drink, sit down and let it happen.', 'Fewer than six wet nappies a day after day five. No weight gain, or continued loss after two weeks. A baby too sleepy to wake for feeds. Dark urine or brick-dust coloured stains in the nappy after day four. Feeding that hurts every time, or cracked bleeding nipples: pain almost always means a latch problem, and a latch problem is fixable with the right help.', 'A lactation consultant is worth the money and most Indian cities now have them. Ask your paediatrician for a referral rather than working it out from videos.', 'Your baby is being fed and that is what matters. Follow the tin\'s measurements exactly, never dilute to make it last, and use boiled cooled water. Fed is the goal.'],
