    paragraphs: ['Six and a half is a reasonable age to start pocket money properly. The habits set now hold surprisingly well.', 'Small, regular, predictable. A fixed amount on a fixed day.', 'Keep it separate from behaviour and from ordinary chores. Money as a reward for good behaviour makes behaviour transactional; money for basic contribution sets up bargaining.', 'Give it in cash. Physical money is comprehensible at six in a way that digital is not, and most children now grow up seeing payment happen invisibly.', 'Spend, save, share. Decide the split together: even a simple one third each.', 'The save jar needs a goal they chose and can picture. Saving toward nothing is meaningless at six.', 'The share jar is worth keeping. Children this age are naturally generous and it is far easier to build the habit now than to introduce it at fifteen.', 'This is the whole point. Buying something that breaks immediately, or spending everything and then wanting something better, teaches more than any explanation. It is a cheap lesson now and an expensive one later.', 'Resist rescuing them. Do not top up when they run out.', 'Six-year-olds can understand that money is limited, that it comes from work, and that choices involve trade-offs.', 'Answer questions about family finances honestly at their level, without anxiety. "We have enough for what we need, and we choose carefully about extra things."', 'Avoid "we can\'t afford it" as a reflex, said with stress. "We\'re not buying that today" or "that\'s not what we\'re spending money on" is truer and less alarming.', 'They will notice what other families have and say so. Answer plainly and without shame or judgement in either direction.', 'Separate one-off tasks beyond ordinary contribution can be paid: washing the car, a big sorting job. It teaches the connection between work and money without commodifying basic family participation.', 'The child who learns at six that money runs out, that saving works, and that choosing one thing means not having another has a genuine advantage later. Almost none of that is taught in school.'],
